pes": "{'Hypercalcemia'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.626}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2.1", "label": "Squamous Cell Carcinoma (Lung/Head and Neck) with PTHrP Secretion", "posterior": 0.365, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.2", "B3", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=67%, source=both]\n\nMedication-Induced Hypercalcemia vs Squamous Cell Carcinoma (Lung/Head and Neck) with PTHrP Secretion:\n  Favours first:  agitation, altered level of consciousness, attention deficit, confusion, delusions\n  Favours second: autosomal recessive inheritance, horn-like growth, itching, nail changes, numbness\n\nMedication-Induced Hypercalcemia vs Hematologic Malignancy (Lymphoma/Leukemia) with Hypercalcemia:\n  Favours first:  agitation, altered level of consciousness, attention deficit, delusions, disorientation\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nMedication-Induced Hypercalcemia vs Metastatic Breast/Ovarian Cancer with Bone Involvement:\n  Favours first:  agitation, altered level of consciousness, attention deficit, confusion, delusions\n  Favours second: bone pain, constipation, difficulty walking, fatigue, hypercalcemia\n\nSquamous Cell Carcinoma (Lung/Head and Neck) with PTHrP Secretion vs Hematologic Malignancy (Lymphoma/Leukemia) with Hypercalcemia:\n  Favours first:  autosomal recessive inheritance, horn-like growth, itching, nail changes, numbness\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nSquamous Cell Carcinoma (Lung/Head and Neck) with PTHrP Secretion vs Metastatic Breast/Ovarian Cancer with Bone Involvement:\n  Favours first:  autosomal recessive inheritance, horn-like growth, itching, nail changes, open sore that won't heal\n  Favours second: bone pain, constipation, difficulty walking, fatigue, hypercalcemia\n\nHematologic Malignancy (Lymphoma/Leukemia) with Hypercalcemia vs Metastatic Breast/Ovarian Cancer with Bone Involvement:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: difficulty walking, hypercalcemia, loss of appetite, numbness, pathologic fracture\n\nRelated to Squamous Cell Carcinoma (Lung/Head and Neck) with PTHrP Secretion: adenosquamous carcinoma, anal canal squamous cell carcinoma, anal carcinoma\n\nRelated to Hematologic Malignancy (Lymphoma/Leukemia) with Hypercalcemia: hypercalcemia disease, paraneoplastic syndrome\n\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.84):]\n  Source: Familial benign hypercalcemia (hypocalciuric hypercalcemia). Clinical and pathogenetic studies in 21 families. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Objectives: Identify the clinical features of familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia.Evaluate patients with presentations consistent with familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia.Implement the recommended m\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.86):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Differential Diagnosis\n  The following conditions should be considered when evaluating hypercalcemia:\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.89):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Differential Diagnosis\n  The following conditions should be considered when evaluating hypercalcemia:\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.91):]\n  Source: Delayed drug hypersensitivity reactions. > History and Physical\n  Around 95% of patients experience involvement of the lungs or thoracic lymph nodes, with common symptoms such as cough, shortness of breath, fatigue, and chest pain. Other frequent features include fe\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  weight loss of 5 pounds recently → malignancy-related hypercalcemia → Hematologic Malignancy (Lymphoma/Leukemia) with Hypercalcemia\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for hypercalcemia (serum calcium) and consider hematologic malignancy as a paraneoplastic cause\n  worsening abdominal pain and poorly controlled GERD → hypercalcemia-induced gastrointestinal dysfunction → Medication-Induced Hypercalcemia\n    (frequency: frequent)"}
